    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Suleiman asked Sinan for a mosque of his own after the Şehzade Mosque, intending one that would surpass all those built by his predecessors and represent the pre-eminence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</li><li>Its prayer hall follows the dome-and-semi-dome plan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> Mosque, but Sinan hid most of the buttresses in the walls so that the outside rises towards the central dome.</li><li>The mosque stood at the centre of a külliye of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, a hospital, a public kitchen and other buildings, and behind its qibla wall lie the octagonal mausoleums of Suleiman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrem-sultan">Hürrem Sultan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The four minarets have ten balconies in all, which is said to reflect that Suleiman I was the 10th Ottoman sultan.</p></aside>
    </section>

